WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.640
OK, let's get into audience questions for&nbsp;
the week. David, now, with more hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.160
can we finally say what George W. Bush's&nbsp;
legacy is and how does it compare to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.120
you thought it would be when he left office?
This is a really interesting question. I actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:23.600
see George W. Bush's legacy now, many years out&nbsp;
from his presidency, as exactly what I believed it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.400
would be when George W. Bush left office. Now,&nbsp;
you could make the case. It just hasn't been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:34.240
enough time. We need to wait even more time to&nbsp;
know what George W. Bush's real long term legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:41.440
is going to be, but sort of around the edges&nbsp;
from the point of view of of tone rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:48.080
substance. George W. Bush was known for being sort&nbsp;
of the dumb president, not particularly bright.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:55.920
I think the Trump presidency has put that in&nbsp;
context, like at least George W. Bush read books.&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.800
You know, he was dumb ish, but&nbsp;
not as dumb as Donald Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:09.360
on policy and failures of George W. Bush's&nbsp;
legacy when he left office was that of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:17.840
a war criminal who went to war with the wrong&nbsp;
country after the 9/11 attacks, invading Iraq&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:24.720
and starting the disaster that we now know of and&nbsp;
even have much agreement on from many Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:34.720
as to that war. Also in the post 9/11 era, George&nbsp;
W. Bush's legacy is the Patriot Act and the sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:43.040
of start of a progressive set, not progressive&nbsp;
politically, but a growing and advancing set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:50.480
restrictions on privacy of many kinds, including&nbsp;
via technological means. The failure on Katrina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:58.160
certainly Hurricane Katrina, one of the&nbsp;
main legacy elements of George W. Bush.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:07.040
And that's really what we believed when George W.&nbsp;
Bush left office. And this is interesting from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:13.680
standpoint of being able to tell versus not being&nbsp;
able to tell legacy relatively quickly. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:22.880
I think the question would be if you went back&nbsp;
to FDR, did we know FDR, his legacy, right. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.880
when he was no longer in office? I think looking&nbsp;
at the contemporaneous writing you sort of did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:38.560
getting the U.S. and the world beyond World&nbsp;
War two and the New Deal that that was widely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:45.360
written about at the time and I would argue is&nbsp;
still the bulk of FDR legacy. Abraham Lincoln,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:51.600
you go back to Abraham Lincoln and, quote, freeing&nbsp;
the slaves, being part of the union around Civil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:57.600
War times. I don't know if the term legacy was&nbsp;
being used at the time of Lincoln's assassination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.320
but certainly it was clear that that was a big&nbsp;
deal. Not everybody liked it, but that that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:09.920
a big deal. So I do think that the benefit of&nbsp;
hindsight is useful in thinking about what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.520
the legacy of a president and the more distance&nbsp;
you get from it, the more important and useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:22.480
that that can be. But with George W. Bush, at&nbsp;
least the his legacy was pretty clear right away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:32.640
And now, as we are 13 years out from George&nbsp;
W. Bush, it has remained that way. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.720
if there are things I'm missing that you think&nbsp;
are part of George W. Bush's legacy, let me know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.600
And then the question becomes, what is Donald&nbsp;
Trump's legacy going to be? And not good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.840
I think is how I would characterize it. But we'll&nbsp;
leave the deeper analysis for another moment.

